1
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:10,280
A rectangular cake is a cuboid, but not a cube.

2
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:12,710
Its edges are all different lengths.

3
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:18,860
It must be cut by one straight slice, into two halves that are exact mirror images.

4
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:21,450
How many different ways can you do it?

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,040
Pause the video and work it out.

6
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:32,240
3 ways: across the length, across the width, or across the height, each time through the middle.

7
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:37,500
Remember: a rectangle has 2 lines of symmetry, and a square has 4.

8
00:00:37,550 --> 00:00:44,650
A plane of symmetry is a flat cut that splits a solid into two congruent halves that are mirror images.

9
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:48,600
A cuboid with three different edge lengths has 3.

10
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:49,270
Your turn.

11
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:54,680
How many planes of symmetry does a square-based cuboid have, if it is not a cube?

12
00:00:54,730 --> 00:00:57,890
Pause the video and try it.

13
00:00:57,940 --> 00:00:58,040
5.

14
00:00:58,090 --> 00:01:03,620
The square end has 4 lines of symmetry, giving 4 planes along the length.

15
00:01:03,670 --> 00:01:06,230
Plus 1 plane halfway along.

16
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:11,210
A cube has 9: 3 parallel to the faces, and 6 through opposite edges.

17
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:14,030
A cylinder has infinitely many.

18
00:01:14,080 --> 00:01:15,330
Here is the method.

19
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:20,170
Step 1: look at the end shape, and count its lines of symmetry.

20
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:24,290
Step 2: each line gives a plane along the length.

21
00:01:24,340 --> 00:01:28,630
Step 3: if the ends match, add a plane halfway along.

22
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:32,600
Step 4: imagine a mirror on each plane, to check.

23
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:36,580
A prism with isosceles triangle ends, not equilateral.

24
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:39,240
The triangle has 1 line of symmetry.

25
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:42,080
Plus 1 halfway along: 2 planes.

26
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:46,310
A prism with equilateral triangle ends: 3 lines, plus 1.

27
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,840
That makes 4.

28
00:01:47,890 --> 00:01:48,530
Your turn.

29
00:01:48,580 --> 00:01:52,940
How many planes of symmetry does a regular hexagonal prism have?

30
00:01:52,990 --> 00:01:55,850
Pause and work it out.

31
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:59,090
A regular hexagon has 6 lines of symmetry.

32
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:01,490
Plus 1 halfway along: 7.

33
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:07,300
A cone has infinitely many: any plane through its point and the centre of its base.

34
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:11,200
Find the planes of symmetry of a 2 by 2 by 5 cuboid.

35
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:12,100
Kit answers.

36
00:02:12,150 --> 00:02:14,140
Which line is wrong?

37
00:02:14,190 --> 00:02:15,280
Line 2 is wrong.

38
00:02:15,330 --> 00:02:19,040
He forgot the plane halfway along the length.

39
00:02:19,090 --> 00:02:21,690
4 plus 1 is 5 planes.

40
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:25,390
Most cars have one plane of symmetry, down the middle.

41
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,230
Designers often model one half, then reflect it.

42
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:30,480
One last question.

43
00:02:30,530 --> 00:02:35,060
Does a sphere have infinitely many planes of symmetry, or 1 plane?

44
00:02:35,110 --> 00:02:38,330
Pause the video and choose.

45
00:02:38,380 --> 00:02:39,450
Infinitely many.

46
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:44,610
Any plane through the centre cuts it into two mirror-image halves.

47
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:50,050
To remember: a plane of symmetry cuts a solid into mirror-image halves.

48
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:53,940
Cuboid 3, square-based cuboid 5, cube 9.

49
00:02:53,990 --> 00:02:57,060
Prism: the end shape's lines, plus 1.

50
00:02:57,110 --> 00:03:00,900
Cylinders, cones and spheres: infinitely many.

51
00:03:00,950 --> 00:03:06,390
Great work! Now practise lesson 14.3 on Camday.
